  <header class="qa-header">
    <p class="qa-pillar">Species Sub-page · SPC·09n</p>
    <h1>Little Raven Nesting: Breeding Season and Nest Sites</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Little Raven (<em>Corvus mellori</em>) nests in tall trees — typically large eucalypts — in open woodland and montane forest across south-eastern Australia. Clutch size is typically 4–5 eggs. Breeding season runs approximately July–November (Southern Hemisphere late-winter to spring), with egg-laying peaking August–September.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Nest Sites</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Little Raven places its nest in a tree despite being one of the most nomadic of the Australian corvids outside the breeding season:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Primary sites:</strong> fork or stout lateral branch in the upper canopy of a tall eucalyptus. In alpine areas of Victoria and southern New South Wales, it uses snow gums and other subalpine trees at elevations up to around 1,800 m.</li>
      <li><strong>Open country:</strong> in farmland and mallee, uses isolated paddock trees or shelter-belt trees when taller stands are unavailable.</li>
      <li><strong>Breeding territories:</strong> pairs defend nesting territories; outside the season, individuals join large nomadic flocks that follow invertebrate eruptions and grain harvests across south-eastern Australia.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Nest Structure and Eggs</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The nest is a large stick platform, lined with bark, grass, wool, and hair. Clutch: typically 4–5 eggs. Eggs are pale blue-green to greenish-grey, blotched with brown and grey — typical of Australasian corvids. Incubation: approximately 20 days, mainly by the female. Chicks fledge at approximately 5–6 weeks; juvenile birds subsequently join nomadic flocks, beginning the highly mobile non-breeding phase of the annual cycle.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Breeding Season</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Breeding timing aligns with the Southern Hemisphere spring:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Territory establishment:</strong> July.</li>
      <li><strong>Egg-laying:</strong> August–October (peak August–September).</li>
      <li><strong>Fledging:</strong> October–November.</li>
      <li><strong>Post-breeding nomadism:</strong> flocks reform from November onward, wandering hundreds of kilometres in pursuit of food resources.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Alpine-breeding individuals may start slightly later than lowland birds due to snow cover delaying territory access.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Related Pages</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/species/little-raven/">Little Raven — species hub</a></li>
